kube_controller_manager manages various controller in kubernetes

controller is process that continously monitor the status of various components withint hte system and work towards bringing the whole system towards desired functioning state

node controller is responsible for monitoring the state of nodes and taking necessary actions to keep applications running
it checks the status of nodes every 5s
if it stops receiving health checks from a node then node is marked unreachable
it waits for 40s before marking as unreachable
after node is marked unreachable it gives 5mins to come back, if it doesn't, it removes the pods in that node and provisons them on healthy node if th pods are part of replicaset 

replication controller,
it is responsible to monitoring the status of replica set and ensuring that desired no of pods are available at all the times within the set
if a pod dies it creates another one 

there many more controller available in kubernetes

these controllers ae the brain in kubernetes

all these controllers are packaged into a single process called kubernetes-controller manager

when you install kubernetes-controller manager the diff controllers get installed as well

manual way setup
download the kube_controller_manager in kubernetes release page 
extract it and run it as a service

here it will mention the node monitor period, node monitor grace period, pod eviction timeout
additional option called controller, you can use it to specify whoch controllers to enable
by default all controllers are enabled

viewing the kube_controller_manager server
if it setup manually
cat/etc/systemd/system/kube-controller-manager.service

with kube admin tool
kube admin tool deploys kube_controller_manager as a pod in the kube system namespace on the master node
you can see the option of kube_controller_manager is the pod definition file located in 
/etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube_controller_manager.yaml

you can view running process of kube_controller manager using
ps -aux | grep  kube-controller-manager

